sv_inline.h: the SV body-size fields are U16, so a 16-byte-pointer body still fits.

struct body_details describes every SV body's size in a U8. Where a pointer is
16 bytes two bodies outgrow that: XPVIO is 256 bytes and truncates to 0, and
regexp is 320 and truncates to 64. The arena would then carve slots smaller than
the bodies they are for, silently. clang reports both
(-Wconstant-conversion) and the build ignored the warnings. The three size
fields become U16; every use is arithmetic on a size (sv_inline.h:315-317,
461-469), so nothing else changes. Natively the values are unchanged and fit
either width.

--- a/sv_inline.h	2023-11-28 19:57:24.000000000 +0800
+++ b/sv_inline.h	2026-09-26 14:57:22.689303999 +0800
@@ -122,9 +122,13 @@
 typedef struct xpvhv_with_aux XPVHV_WITH_AUX;
 
 struct body_details {
-    U8 body_size;      /* Size to allocate  */
-    U8 copy;           /* Size of structure to copy (may be shorter)  */
-    U8 offset;         /* Size of unalloced ghost fields to first alloced field*/
+    /* capstone: U16, not U8. Where a pointer is 16 bytes these sizes outgrow a
+       byte -- XPVIO is 256 and truncated to 0, regexp 320 truncated to 64 -- and
+       the arena then hands out a slot smaller than the body it is for. The
+       compiler says so (-Wconstant-conversion) and the build ignored it. */
+    U16 body_size;     /* Size to allocate  */
+    U16 copy;          /* Size of structure to copy (may be shorter)  */
+    U16 offset;        /* Size of unalloced ghost fields to first alloced field*/
     PERL_BITFIELD8 type : 4;        /* We have space for a sanity check. */
     PERL_BITFIELD8 cant_upgrade : 1;/* Cannot upgrade this type */
     PERL_BITFIELD8 zero_nv : 1;     /* zero the NV when upgrading from this */
